{
  "concept": "When a rational function's numerator degree is exactly one more than its denominator's, long division splits it into a linear part plus a vanishing remainder, so the graph blows up at the vertical asymptote x = 1 while its ends settle onto the slant asymptote y = 3x + 1.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrowheads at both ends, integer ticks and labels from -5 to 5, axis label 'x' at right end",
    "Vertical y-axis with arrowheads at both ends, even ticks and labels from -12 to 12, axis label 'y' at top",
    "Solid dark-blue curve, left branch: enters lower-left hugging the teal dashed line, rises to a shallow local maximum near (0.18, -0.9) just below the x-axis, then plunges down off the bottom of the frame as x approaches 1 from the left",
    "Solid dark-blue curve, right branch: descends from the top of the frame just right of x = 1, turns at a local minimum near (1.82, 8.9), then climbs to the upper right, closing on the teal dashed line",
    "Dashed orange vertical line at x = 1 spanning the full frame height (the vertical asymptote)",
    "Orange label 'x = 1' placed to the right of the orange line, low in the frame near y = -9",
    "Dashed teal line of slope 3 through (0, 1), drawn from lower-left to upper-right corner of the frame (the slant asymptote)",
    "Teal two-line label 'Slant asymptote / y = 3x + 1' placed above and right of the origin, near (2.5, 5.5)",
    "Probe point marker on the curve (movable dot) with its x-value readout",
    "Vertical guide segment from the probe point to the slant asymptote, annotated with the gap |f(x) - (3x+1)|",
    "Horizontal guide segment from the probe point to the line x = 1, annotated with the distance |x - 1|",
    "Optional readout box showing f(x) = (3x² - 2x + 1)/(x - 1) = 3x + 1 + 2/(x - 1)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "xProbe",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Moving a point along the curve makes the two gaps compete: as x nears 1 the remainder term 2/(x-1) explodes and the curve tears away from the slant line, while for large |x| that same term shrinks to nothing and the curve merges with y = 3x + 1.",
      "label": "Probe x-value",
      "range": [-4.5, 4.5, 0.05],
      "default": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The dashed lines are not part of the function — hiding and restoring them shows that the curve alone still bends toward two invisible boundaries, and that the asymptotes alone are just guides drawn from the long-division quotient and the zero of the denominator.",
      "label": "What is drawn",
      "options": ["Curve only", "Asymptotes only", "Combined"],
      "default": "Combined"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The curve by itself",
      "narration": "Only the solid blue graph of f(x) = (3x² - 2x + 1)/(x - 1) is shown, in two separate branches. Notice that both branches seem to be steered by something invisible: they run off vertically somewhere near x = 1 and straighten into a steep line at both far ends.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Curve only", "xProbe": 2.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The two boundary lines",
      "narration": "Now only the guides appear: the orange dashed vertical line x = 1, where the denominator is zero, and the teal dashed line y = 3x + 1, the quotient of the long division. Neither line is part of the function — they are the boundaries the function negotiates.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Asymptotes only", "xProbe": 2.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Far out: the slant asymptote wins",
      "narration": "With everything drawn, drag the probe out to x = 4. The vertical gap between the curve and the teal line is tiny, because the remainder 2/(x - 1) is nearly zero — the end behavior is linear, not horizontal.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Combined", "xProbe": 4.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close in: the vertical asymptote wins",
      "narration": "Slide the probe to x = 1.1. The horizontal distance to x = 1 is small, so 2/(x - 1) is huge and the point rockets up the frame, far from the slant line. Local blow-up is controlled entirely by the vertical asymptote.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Combined", "xProbe": 1.1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Cross to the left branch",
      "narration": "Move the probe to x = -3. The mirror behavior appears on the other side: the curve again presses against the teal line from below, while approaching x = 1 from the left would send it downward off the frame. Same two boundaries, opposite signs.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Combined", "xProbe": -3.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Writing f(x) as 3x + 1 + 2/(x - 1) explains the whole picture at once: the linear piece is the slant asymptote the ends ride on, and the fractional piece is the term that blows up at x = 1 and dies off at infinity.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single Cartesian plot on white, x from -5 to 5 and y from -12 to 12, thin black axes with arrowheads and the labels 'x' and 'y'. The plotted function is f(x) = (3x² - 2x + 1)/(x - 1), drawn as a thick dark navy-blue curve in two branches with a break at x = 1 (never connect across the asymptote; clip at the frame top and bottom). Left branch: comes from the lower-left corner just above the teal dashed line, peaks at about (0.18, -0.9) just under the x-axis, then falls steeply off the bottom as x → 1⁻. Right branch: drops from the top of the frame just right of x = 1, bottoms out near (1.82, 8.9), then rises toward the upper right approaching the teal line from above. Dashed orange (#E8722C) vertical line at x = 1 spanning the full height, labeled 'x = 1' in orange to the right of the line near y = -9. Dashed teal (#1B7B7B) line y = 3x + 1 across the full frame, labeled in teal on two lines, 'Slant asymptote' above 'y = 3x + 1', placed up and right of the origin around (2.3, 5.5). Probe: a filled blue dot on the curve at the slider's x, a thin vertical dashed connector from the dot to the teal line labeled with the gap value, and a thin horizontal dashed connector from the dot to the orange line labeled with |x − 1|. The slider must skip a small neighborhood of x = 1 (snap the probe to the nearer side) so the point never sits on the pole. In 'Curve only' mode hide both dashed lines and their labels; in 'Asymptotes only' hide the blue curve and the probe; 'Combined' shows everything. Keep a small readout of f(x) = 3x + 1 + 2/(x − 1) with the current numeric value of each term."
}

